 ### Health: `GET /health`
 
 `{ "status": "ok" }` while the service answers.
+
+### Assign a ticket: `PUT /tickets/:id/assignee`
+
+Send `{ "assignee": "Dana" }`, or `{ "assignee": null }` to unassign. Names are trimmed, non-empty single-line text of at most 64 characters. Answers 200 with the ticket. Only open and in-progress tickets allow this operation; other states answer 409 `conflict`. An unchanged assignment leaves no audit entry or notification and keeps `updated`. Each new assignment records `ticket.assigned` in the audit log and enqueues a notification to the new agent. Unassignment sends no notification.
+
+## Import tickets
+
+`POST /tickets/import` takes `{ "tickets": [...] }`, with up to 5000 objects in the same format as
+`POST /tickets`. An empty array succeeds. Each item uses the usual field validation, trimming and priority default.
+On success the response is 201 with `{ "created": 2, "ids": ["T-0001", "T-0002"] }`, in input order.
+An invalid item returns 400 in the usual error envelope, with the first invalid zero-based index in the message
+(for example, `tickets[1]: title must be a string`). The entire import is rejected without consuming ids,
+adding audit entries or enqueueing notifications. Each successful creation has its usual audit entry and requester
+notification, with the actor from `X-Actor`.
+
+The import body limit is the greater of `MAX_BODY_BYTES` and 16 MiB, to accommodate 5000 full-size ticket objects.
+Other routes retain `MAX_BODY_BYTES`. The batch is saved once, so imports do not rewrite the data file per ticket.
+
+With `DUE_TIMES=on`, all ticket responses include `due`, an ISO UTC time fixed at creation. `GET /tickets?overdue=true` selects only open and in-progress tickets strictly past that time, combined with other filters before pagination. `overdue=false` applies no additional filter; other values return 400. With the flag off, due is omitted and the overdue parameter is ignored as before.
